favored.", "watch": "A Mississippi-specific poll showing a far closer race, or a decisive national shift toward Democrats by 2028, would be the main reason to revisit this forecast.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No expert ratings published yet; forecast rests on fundamentals and polls.", "New or vacant seat with no expert ratings; fundamentals only.", "No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings."]}, "overview": "Mississippi remains one of the most Republican-leaning states in presidential politics, having backed the GOP nominee by double digits in every recent cycle. With the incumbent president term-limited, neither party benefits from incumbency, and the race defaults to the state's underlying partisan lean. No public polls of the general election and no expert ratings are available, so the forecast rests on fundamentals.\n\nThe Republican nominee starts with a clear advantage of roughly eleven points. National coverage currently centers on the 2028 primary contests and the 2026 midterms, with no evidence of a Mississippi-specific shift. A Democratic win would require a national wave well beyond anything currently indicated. Uncertainty is wide this far from Election Day, but the state's structural lean makes an upset unlikely without a major change in the political environment.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.1069, "margin": -11.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.1631, "margin": -11.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.153, "margin": -11.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.1578, "margin": -11.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.153, "margin": -11.0}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.1586, "margin": -11.0}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.1588, "margin": -11.0}]}